// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > add-key 1`] = `
"oip add-key [options]

Makes a new key of everyday work, and adds it to the mind's keys

The keys the mind has stay. The new key is sealed in the mind's directory.
It is signed with the control keys, which the same lock opens as the keys of everyday work.

Options:
  --label <name>  A name for the key, of at most 64 characters: where it is used, say

Example:
  oip add-key --label laptop
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > collective create 1`] = `
"oip collective create [options]

Founds a collective with other minds: drafts its first event, and signs it as a member

The collective exists once every founder has signed its first event, and the event is
published. Each founder signs with \`oip collective sign\`, and the last adds --publish.
The event is held by this mind, under the ID this prints. Send it to the others: --send-to.
It is signed with an invocation key, which the same lock opens as the keys of everyday work.

Options:
  --member <address>   A founding member besides this mind. It may be given several times
  --threshold <count>  How many members act for it: a majority of them
  --host <origin>      A host that will serve the collective's records
  --out <file>         Write the event to this file too, with the proofs it has
  --send-to <address>  Send it to this mind, sealed. It may be given several times

Example:
  oip collective create --member did:oip:bciqexample --member did:oip:bciqother --threshold 2 --send-to did:oip:bciqexample
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > collective sign 1`] = `
"oip collective sign <record> [options]

Signs, as a member of a collective, one of its identity events or a record in its name

The record is named by the ID of one the mind holds, as \`oip inbox\` shows it, or by a file.
Read what you sign. An event changes who the collective is, and a key signs one event for
each position of a collective's log, and no other. A record is the collective's once the
number of members it acts by have signed it.
The proofs of every copy this mind has received are put together. Published, a log goes
to the resolvers this mind trusts: anyone may publish a log, which stands by its signatures.

Options:
  --publish            An event: publish the collective's log with it. A record: put it on this mind's feed
  --out <file>         Write the event to this file too, with the proofs it has
  --send-to <address>  Send it to this mind, sealed. It may be given several times

Example:
  oip collective sign bciqexample --publish
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > commit 1`] = `
"oip commit [commitment] [options]

Makes a promise between minds: writes a Commitment, or signs one another wrote

A Commitment binds each mind whose signature on it counts, and is accepted once every
debtor and creditor has signed. Read what you sign: it cannot be taken back, only answered
with a Receipt.
Given a Commitment, by the ID of one the mind holds or by a file, this signs it as it is.
Otherwise it writes one, from an Offer or from the options, and signs that.

Options:
  --offer <offer>           Take up this Offer: its author owes, this mind is owed, on the Offer's terms
  --debtor <address>        A mind that owes what is promised. \`me\` is this mind
  --creditor <address>      A mind that is owed it. \`me\` is this mind
  --witness <address>       A mind that witnesses it
  --promise <text>          What is promised, in words
  --deadline <time>         By when, as 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z
  --price <amount>          What is paid, as 120 or 99.50
  --currency <code>         What the price is counted in: USD
  --payer <address>         Who pays. Default: the first creditor
  --payee <address>         Who is paid. Default: the first debtor
  --resolves <record>       An Intent or an Offer it resolves: an ID or a file
  --closes-intent           Once accepted, the Intent it resolves is satisfied
  --for <address>           The mind it is signed for, in place of this one: a collective this mind is a member of
  --authority <delegation>  A delegation that lets this mind sign for another: an ID or a file. A chain, root first
  --charter <charter>       The charter that gives this mind a role in the collective it signs for
  --publish                 Put it on the mind's public feed, at its hosts, where anyone may read it
  --send-to <address>       Send it to this mind, sealed. It may be given several times

Example:
  oip commit --offer bciqexample --send-to did:oip:bciqexample
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > completions 1`] = `
"oip completions <shell>

Prints a script that teaches a shell the commands and their options

The shell is bash, zsh or fish. Load what this prints when the shell starts:
  bash:  source <(oip completions bash)
  zsh:   source <(oip completions zsh)
  fish:  oip completions fish | source

Example:
  oip completions bash
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > delegate 1`] = `
"oip delegate <delegate> [options]

Lets another mind sign for this one, within limits: signs a Delegation

What the delegate signs within the delegation binds this mind. A policy constrains only
what it selects: constrain every term that matters, and exclude the delegate as a party.
No longer than its purpose needs: expiry is what bounds a revocation that is missed.
With --authority, the chain that lets this mind act, it passes part of that on.
Give it to the delegate, who names it in what it signs: --send-to.

Options:
  --command <command>       What it may do: /oip/sign/Commitment, /oip/sign/Offer, /oip/send…
  --days <count>            For how many days from now
  --expires <time>          Or until when, as 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z
  --not-before <time>       From when. Default: from now
  --policy <file>           What must hold of each record it signs so: a file with a JSON array of statements
  --authority <delegation>  A delegation that lets this mind sign for another: an ID or a file. A chain, root first
  --send-to <address>       Send it to this mind, sealed. It may be given several times
  --publish                 Put it on the mind's public feed, at its hosts, where anyone may read it

Example:
  oip delegate did:oip:bciqexample --command /oip/sign/Commitment --days 30 --policy policy.json --send-to did:oip:bciqexample
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > demo 1`] = `
"oip demo [options]

Runs a network on this machine: a resolver, a host and an indexer, until it is stopped

It is for trying the protocol with nothing else to hand. What it holds is kept, and is
there when it is started again. It listens on this machine only.

Options:
  --network <path>  Where the network keeps what it holds. Default: ~/.oip-demo

Example:
  oip demo
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > doctor 1`] = `
"oip doctor

Looks at the mind's directory and its services, and says what is well and what is not

It changes nothing, and unlocks no key: no passphrase is asked for.
Each line is one thing it looked at. \`warn\` is what works and should be seen to. The
command ends with 1 if anything fails: the mind then cannot do its work.

Example:
  oip doctor
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > find 1`] = `
"oip find [options]

Finds minds by what they can do: asks the indexers, and verifies what they name

An indexer's answer is a claim. Every record it names is fetched from its author's hosts and
verified here before it is shown, and what does not check out is told, with why.
The ranking is the indexer's own, and so are its reasons.
Asked from a mind's directory, what is found is kept there: \`oip commit --offer\` takes one up.

Options:
  --want <text>            What is wanted, in words
  --tag <namespace:value>  A tag a result must have, as domain:legal. oasf:nlp/* is any tag that begins so
  --kind <kind>            What to find: participant, offer or intent. Default: participant
  --limit <count>          The most results: 1 to 100. Default: 20
  --local                  Ask the network \`oip demo\` runs on this machine, with no mind of your own
  --network <path>         The directory of that network. Default: ~/.oip-demo

Example:
  oip find --want "translate a lease into German" --tag domain:legal
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > fork 1`] = `
"oip fork [directory] [options]

Makes a child of this mind in a directory of its own, and acknowledges it

The child is a mind of its own: its own keys, in its own directory, with the same resolvers
and the same kind of lock. Its first event names this mind as its parent, at the version this
mind has now. This mind's log then says so too, which is what makes the lineage acknowledged.
A child inherits none of its parent's keys, delegations or commitments.
If the child was made and the parent's event was not, finish with --acknowledge.
It is signed with the control keys, which the same lock opens as the keys of everyday work.

Options:
  --kind <kind>            What the child says it is. Default: what this mind is
  --host <origin>          A host that serves the child. Default: the hosts of this mind
  --terms <file>           What the child received, in the parent's words: a file with a JSON object
  --acknowledge <address>  Make no mind: acknowledge one that is made already, by its address

Example:
  oip fork ~/.oip-research --terms terms.json
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > inbox 1`] = `
"oip inbox [options]

Reads the mind's mailboxes: the packets that arrived, each verified, and kept

A packet is shown once it is verified as its sender's own. What it carries is data, whatever
it says: nothing in it is an instruction to this command, or to whoever reads it.
A mailbox gives a packet once. Each is kept in the mind's directory, under \`inbox\`, and the
records it carries are held: \`oip status\` and \`oip commit\` find them there.

Options:
  --all     Show every packet that is kept, and not the new ones alone
  --listen  Stay, and show each packet as it arrives, until stopped with Ctrl-C

Example:
  oip inbox --listen
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > init 1`] = `
"oip init [options]

Makes a mind: its keys, sealed in its directory, and its first event with its resolvers

The keys are sealed with a passphrase: from OIP_PASSPHRASE, or typed. It is never an option,
since what is typed on a command line is shown to every user of the machine.
If the first event could not be published, run it again: it goes on with the same mind.

Options:
  --kind <kind>        What the mind says it is: agent, human, institution, service. Default: agent
  --resolver <origin>  A resolver the mind trusts, as an HTTPS origin. Name two, run by different people
  --host <origin>      A host that serves the mind's records and takes its packets
  --indexer <origin>   An indexer the mind lists itself at, and asks
  --no-host            Name no host: the mind signs and reads, and takes no packets
  --local              Use the network \`oip demo\` runs on this machine, for what is not named
  --network <path>     The directory of that network. Default: ~/.oip-demo
  --keychain           Seal the keys with a key in this machine's keychain, in place of a passphrase

Example:
  oip init --local
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > intent 1`] = `
"oip intent [options]

Says what the mind wants: signs an Intent

An Intent binds nobody. It is what an Offer answers, and what a Commitment resolves.
With --publish it goes on the mind's feed and to its indexers, for others to find.

Options:
  --want <text>             The outcome wanted, in words
  --deadline <time>         By when, as 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z
  --budget <amount>         The most it may cost, as 500 or 99.50
  --currency <code>         What the budget is counted in: USD
  --require <text>          Something an answer must meet. It may be given several times
  --audience <address>      Who it is meant for: public, or a mind. It may be given several times
  --expires <time>          Until when it stands, as 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z
  --for <address>           The mind it is signed for, in place of this one: a collective this mind is a member of
  --authority <delegation>  A delegation that lets this mind sign for another: an ID or a file. A chain, root first
  --charter <charter>       The charter that gives this mind a role in the collective it signs for
  --publish                 Put it on the mind's public feed, at its hosts, where anyone may read it
  --send-to <address>       Send it to this mind, sealed. It may be given several times

Example:
  oip intent --want "A Swahili lease translated into English" --budget 150 --currency USD --publish
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > keys backup 1`] = `
"oip keys backup <file> [options]

Writes the mind's keys to a file, sealed with a passphrase of its own

A backup is the one form in which private keys leave the mind's directory. Nothing in it
can be read without its passphrase: from OIP_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE, or typed. Choose one of its
own, and keep it and the file apart from this machine.
Back up again after every change of the mind's identity (\`oip rotate\`, \`add-key\`,
\`revoke-key\`, \`fork\`): each makes a control key the change after it will need. If that key
is lost, the mind's identity can never change again.

Options:
  --replace  Write over a file that is there

Example:
  oip keys backup ~/safe/oip-keys.backup
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > keys restore 1`] = `
"oip keys restore <file> [options]

Makes the mind's directory again from a backup of its keys

The directory must hold no keys yet. The backup is opened with its own passphrase, from
OIP_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE or typed, and the keys are sealed afresh for this machine.
A backup holds the keys as they were when it was made. If the mind's identity has changed
since, the control keys it needs now are not in it: this says so, and the mind then signs
and reads as before, but its identity can change no more. A later backup would mend that.
What the mind's client remembered is not in a backup: its sessions do not go on.

Options:
  --resolver <origin>  A resolver the mind trusts, as an HTTPS origin. Name two, run by different people
  --indexer <origin>   An indexer the mind lists itself at, and asks
  --local              Use the network \`oip demo\` runs on this machine, for what is not named
  --network <path>     The directory of that network. Default: ~/.oip-demo
  --keychain           Seal the keys with a key in this machine's keychain, in place of a passphrase

Example:
  oip keys restore ~/safe/oip-keys.backup --resolver https://resolver.example
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > offer 1`] = `
"oip offer [options]

Says what the mind provides, and on what terms: signs an Offer

An Offer binds nobody until a Commitment takes it up: \`oip commit --offer\`.
With --publish it goes on the mind's feed and to its indexers, for others to find.

Options:
  --provides <text>         What is provided, in words
  --summary <text>          The terms in a line. Default: what is provided
  --price <amount>          What it costs, as 120 or 99.50
  --currency <code>         What the price is counted in: USD
  --delivery-by <time>      By when it is delivered
  --quantity <count>        How many times it can be taken up
  --condition <text>        A condition of the terms. It may be given several times
  --answers <intent>        The Intent it answers: the ID of one the mind holds, or a file
  --capability <id>         The capability of the mind's profile it is an offer of
  --audience <address>      Who it is meant for: public, or a mind. It may be given several times
  --expires <time>          Until when it stands, as 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z
  --for <address>           The mind it is signed for, in place of this one: a collective this mind is a member of
  --authority <delegation>  A delegation that lets this mind sign for another: an ID or a file. A chain, root first
  --charter <charter>       The charter that gives this mind a role in the collective it signs for
  --publish                 Put it on the mind's public feed, at its hosts, where anyone may read it
  --send-to <address>       Send it to this mind, sealed. It may be given several times

Example:
  oip offer --provides "An English translation of a lease" --price 120 --currency USD --publish
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > publish location 1`] = `
"oip publish location [options]

Publishes where the mind takes packets, and whether it takes them now

The mind's endpoints are an inbox and a mailbox at each of its hosts, unless its record
before said otherwise. A location record is good for a time: publish it again before then.

Options:
  --status <status>      active, paused or migrating. Default: as before, or else active
  --resume-after <time>  When a mind that is paused means to be back, as 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z
  --ttl <seconds>        How long the record is good for: 60 to 86400. Default: as before, or else an hour

Example:
  oip publish location --status paused --resume-after 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > publish profile 1`] = `
"oip publish profile [options]

Publishes what the mind is and can do: its profile, which others find it by

The profile takes the place of the one before it: say all of it each time.
A file may hold name, summary, capabilities, actsFor, interfaces, languages and attestations,
as Layer 1, Section 16 has them. An option says its part in place of the file's.

Options:
  --name <name>              The mind's name. Anyone can take any name: it identifies nobody
  --summary <text>           What the mind is, in a sentence or two
  --capability <id=summary>  Something the mind can do: a short ID, and what it is in a sentence
  --tag <namespace:value>    A tag of every capability named here, as domain:legal or lang:de
  --file <file>              A JSON object with the whole profile, for what the options do not say

Example:
  oip publish profile --name Ada --capability translate="Translates legal texts" --tag domain:legal --tag lang:de
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > publish record 1`] = `
"oip publish record <file>

Puts a record on the mind's public feed, at its hosts

The file holds the record, as JSON. It may be another mind's: a commitment this mind is a
party to, say. The feed commits to the record and to the proofs it has now.
An entry of a feed cannot be taken back. A mind with no host has no feed.

Example:
  oip publish record offer.json
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > receipt 1`] = `
"oip receipt <record> [options]

Says what became of a record, usually a Commitment: signs a Receipt

The record is named by the ID of one the mind holds, or by a file.
A Commitment is fulfilled once a mind that is owed it says so, and broken or disputed
by the word of any party. A Receipt is a statement by its author, and no more.

Options:
  --outcome <outcome>       What became of it: accepted, progress, fulfilled, broken, cancelled, disputed, or one of a namespace
  --statement <text>        More of it, in words
  --score <decimal>         A rating from 0 to 1, as 0.9
  --for <address>           The mind it is signed for, in place of this one: a collective this mind is a member of
  --authority <delegation>  A delegation that lets this mind sign for another: an ID or a file. A chain, root first
  --charter <charter>       The charter that gives this mind a role in the collective it signs for
  --publish                 Put it on the mind's public feed, at its hosts, where anyone may read it
  --send-to <address>       Send it to this mind, sealed. It may be given several times

Example:
  oip receipt bciqexample --outcome fulfilled --score 1 --send-to did:oip:bciqexample
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > resolve 1`] = `
"oip resolve <address> [options]

Resolves any mind by its address: fetches its log from the resolvers, and verifies it

The resolvers asked are those of this directory's mind. What they serve is verified here,
every event of it: a resolver is trusted for when it first saw an event, and for nothing else.

Options:
  --aliases         Check each other name the mind lists: it is the mind's only if it points back
  --local           Ask the network \`oip demo\` runs on this machine, with no mind of your own
  --network <path>  The directory of that network. Default: ~/.oip-demo
  --out <file>      Write the mind's bundle to this file: its log, with its location and profile

Example:
  oip resolve did:oip:bciqexample --aliases
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > revoke 1`] = `
"oip revoke <delegation> [options]

Takes a delegation back: signs a Revocation, and puts it on the mind's feed

The delegation is named by the ID of one the mind holds, or by a file.
It takes effect for a verifier from when that verifier first sees it: what the delegate
signed before stands. Send it to whoever relies on the delegate, as well as publishing it.

Options:
  --send-to <address>  Send it to this mind, sealed. It may be given several times

Example:
  oip revoke bciqexample --send-to did:oip:bciqexample
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > revoke-key 1`] = `
"oip revoke-key <key> [options]

Takes a key of everyday work from the mind's keys for good, and destroys it here

What the key signed before stays valid, for a verifier with evidence of when it was signed.
With --compromised-since, what it signed from that time on counts no longer, for everyone
who learns of this event. That cannot be taken back.
A mind keeps at least one key: add another first if this is the last. \`oip show --json\` lists them.

Options:
  --compromised-since <time>  If the key was stolen: from when what it signed is withdrawn, as 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z

Example:
  oip revoke-key z6Mkexample --compromised-since 2026-01-31T12:00:00Z
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > rotate 1`] = `
"oip rotate

Rotates the mind's control keys: the next are revealed, and new ones committed to

Every event of a mind's log reveals the control keys the event before it committed to, and
commits to new ones. This adds an event that does only that. The keys that signed are destroyed.
A key of everyday work is replaced with \`oip add-key\`, and then \`oip revoke-key\`.

Example:
  oip rotate
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > send 1`] = `
"oip send <address>... [options]

Sends a packet to one mind or several: signed, and sealed to each

A packet says something. It binds nobody, whatever its verb: what binds is a record it carries.
A recipient that cannot be reached now is tried again, by \`oip inbox\` and by the next \`oip send\`,
for as long as the packet may be delivered.

Options:
  --text <text>           What to say, as plain text
  --record <file>         A record to carry, as JSON: an offer, a commitment, a delegation
  --verb <verb>           What kind of message it is: inform, request, propose, accept… Default: inform
  --goal <text>           What it is for, in a line
  --reply-to <id>         The packet it answers, by its ID
  --thread <id>           The thread it belongs to
  --expires-in <seconds>  How long it may be delivered: at most 604800, seven days. Default: an hour
  --unsealed              Send it in the clear, signed but readable by the hosts that carry it

Example:
  oip send did:oip:bciqexample --verb request --text "Could you translate a lease by Friday?"
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > show 1`] = `
"oip show

Shows the mind of this directory, as its resolvers have it

It reads, and unlocks no key: no passphrase is asked for.

Example:
  oip show
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > status 1`] = `
"oip status <record>

Says where a record stands: its status, and whom it binds

The record is named by the ID of one the mind holds, or by a file.
A status is computed from the records this mind holds, and from the logs of the minds they
name, fetched now. Another verifier, holding other records, may compute another.

Example:
  oip status bciqexample
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > has help for every command: what it does, its options, and an example to type > verify 1`] = `
"oip verify <what> [options]

Verifies a record, a log, a bundle or a mind, and says why of every verdict

What is verified is named by a file of JSON, by the ID of a record the mind of this
directory holds, or by the address of a mind. A file may hold one record, a bundle, or an
identity log as an array of its events.
Each line is one verdict, with the clause of the specification it rests on. \`unknown\` is
what could not be judged, and \`no\` an answer of no with nothing wrong: a party that has
not signed. The command ends with 1 if anything fails.
A signature is judged by its signer's log as the resolvers serve it now, and counts from
when this mind first obtained it, if it holds the record, or else from now.
It unlocks no key: no passphrase is asked for.

Options:
  --offline         Ask no service: check what can be checked without one
  --local           Ask the network \`oip demo\` runs on this machine, with no mind of your own
  --network <path>  The directory of that network. Default: ~/.oip-demo

Example:
  oip verify commitment.json
"
`;

exports[`oip, asked for nothing or for help > lists its commands, and says how to learn more of one 1`] = `
"oip <command> [options]

A mind of the Open Intelligence Protocol, from a terminal.

Commands:
  init               Makes a mind: its keys, sealed in its directory, and its first event with its resolvers
  show               Shows the mind of this directory, as its resolvers have it
  rotate             Rotates the mind's control keys: the next are revealed, and new ones committed to
  add-key            Makes a new key of everyday work, and adds it to the mind's keys
  revoke-key         Takes a key of everyday work from the mind's keys for good, and destroys it here
  fork               Makes a child of this mind in a directory of its own, and acknowledges it
  publish profile    Publishes what the mind is and can do: its profile, which others find it by
  publish location   Publishes where the mind takes packets, and whether it takes them now
  publish record     Puts a record on the mind's public feed, at its hosts
  resolve            Resolves any mind by its address: fetches its log from the resolvers, and verifies it
  find               Finds minds by what they can do: asks the indexers, and verifies what they name
  send               Sends a packet to one mind or several: signed, and sealed to each
  inbox              Reads the mind's mailboxes: the packets that arrived, each verified, and kept
  intent             Says what the mind wants: signs an Intent
  offer              Says what the mind provides, and on what terms: signs an Offer
  commit             Makes a promise between minds: writes a Commitment, or signs one another wrote
  receipt            Says what became of a record, usually a Commitment: signs a Receipt
  status             Says where a record stands: its status, and whom it binds
  delegate           Lets another mind sign for this one, within limits: signs a Delegation
  revoke             Takes a delegation back: signs a Revocation, and puts it on the mind's feed
  collective create  Founds a collective with other minds: drafts its first event, and signs it as a member
  collective sign    Signs, as a member of a collective, one of its identity events or a record in its name
  verify             Verifies a record, a log, a bundle or a mind, and says why of every verdict
  keys backup        Writes the mind's keys to a file, sealed with a passphrase of its own
  keys restore       Makes the mind's directory again from a backup of its keys
  doctor             Looks at the mind's directory and its services, and says what is well and what is not
  demo               Runs a network on this machine: a resolver, a host and an indexer, until it is stopped
  completions        Prints a script that teaches a shell the commands and their options

Options of every command:
  --dir <path>    The mind's directory. Default: OIP_DIR, or .oip in your home directory
  --json          Print the result as JSON
  --get <member>  Print one member of that JSON alone, as mind or found.0.mind: for a script
  --help          Show this, or a command's own help
  --version       Print the version

Run \`oip <command> --help\` for a command's own options, and an example.
"
`;
